From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Reindert Pelsma <reindert.pelsma@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 21 Aug 2026 12:05:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ui/sdl2: make the pointer grab switch the guest's pointing
 device

*** RUN ON REAL HARDWARE, ONCE, AND REPORTED WORKING BY THE PERSON AT THE
*** SCREEN -- but the instrumentation that would have proved it was armed
*** and never read, because the machine was returned first.  "WHAT THE
*** HARDWARE SAID" below separates the two.  It stays on gpu-untested
*** until someone runs the evtest under "How to actually verify".

Separate from patches/0008 on purpose.  0008 makes the guest visible under SDL;
this makes the grab useful.  Either is worth having without the other, and
neither should be held up by the other's verification.

Ctrl+Alt+G confines the host cursor and changes nothing else, so the guest keeps
receiving whatever kind of coordinates it was receiving before.  With nvkvm's
default virtio-tablet current that is ABSOLUTE coordinates, and a tablet says
"the pointer is at (x, y)", never "it moved by (dx, dy)", so a guest reading
deltas for pointer lock gets nothing.

Concretely, ui/sdl2.c:sdl_hide_cursor() asks for the real lock --
SDL_SetRelativeMouseMode(SDL_TRUE), which SDL2's Wayland backend implements
with zwp_pointer_constraints_v1 + zwp_relative_pointer_manager_v1, the same
mechanism a native game uses -- but only `if (!qemu_input_is_absolute())`.  With
a tablet current that is false, so SDL never enters relative mode and the one
backend that CAN take a real lock does not take it.

So: on grab, activate a relative device; on ungrab, put the absolute one back.
Both directions go through the public interface -- qmp_query_mice() to read the
list, qemu_mouse_set() to activate -- which is exactly what the monitor's
`info mice` and `mouse_set` do, so nothing new is exported from ui/input.c and
the monitor sees the same state.

WHY THIS IS NOT PATCHES/0007 AGAIN.  0007 is the same change for ui/gtk.c and
its header records a negative result: the switch done by hand from the monitor
(`mouse_set <relative index>`, then grab) produced no motion in the guest.  That
result does not carry over, and the reason is measured rather than assumed.  On
that Wayland host QEMU held ZERO fds to wayland-0 -- it was an X11 client under
Xwayland -- gd_grab_pointer() does gdk_seat_grab() plus gdk_device_warp()
re-centring, which asks the compositor for no lock at all, the cursor left the
"grabbed" window freely, and evtest in the guest saw zero REL_X/REL_Y.  There
were no deltas on that host for ANY guest device to receive; which device was
current could not have been the deciding factor.  Under SDL the lock is real, so
the device is the remaining question and this is the answer to it.

That is an argument from two measurements, not a measurement of this patch.
The test that settles it is in "How to actually verify" below.

ORDER MATTERS, in both directions, and is the only subtle thing here:

  * on grab the switch runs BEFORE the cursor decision, because
    sdl_hide_cursor() is what calls SDL_SetRelativeMouseMode() and it tests the
    current device.  Switch after it and the lock is never requested;
  * on ungrab the restore runs AFTER sdl_show_cursor(), which leaves relative
    mode only while the current device is still relative.  Restore the tablet
    first and that test is false, relative mode is never left, and the pointer
    stays locked with no way out.

RE-ENTRANCY.  qemu_mouse_set() calls qemu_input_handler_activate(), which fires
the mouse-mode notifier synchronously -- and ui/sdl2.c's notifier runs the grab
side effects: going relative makes it sdl_grab_end(), going absolute makes it
absolute_mouse_grab().  Called from inside our own switch, each would undo the
transition in progress, and both act on sdl2_console[0] -- the boot VGA's
window, not the guest's.  A flag set across our two qemu_mouse_set() calls makes
the notifier track the mode and skip the side effects; the guest's own mode
changes still reach it unchanged.  (ui/gtk.c's 0007 gets the same effect from
ordering against ptr_owner.  SDL has no equivalent state, hence the flag.)

WHAT THE HARDWARE SAID.  Same single session as 0008: RTX 4070, driver 595.84,
host session GNOME on WAYLAND, Mint 22.3 guest, `-display sdl,gl=on`, with
virtio-mouse present alongside the tablet.

MEASURED:
  * builds clean --enable-sdl, no warning or error mentioning sdl2;
  * `info mice` on the monitor, before any grab:
        Mouse #2: QEMU PS/2 Mouse
        Mouse #7: vmmouse (absolute)
        Mouse #5: QEMU Virtio Mouse
      * Mouse #4: QEMU Virtio Tablet (absolute)
    which is exactly the precondition this patch exists for: an ABSOLUTE device
    current, so sdl_hide_cursor()'s !qemu_input_is_absolute() test is false and
    SDL_SetRelativeMouseMode() would never be reached.  It also confirms the
    device the switch must find (#5) is present and not current;
  * the guest's relative node is /dev/input/event6 ("QEMU Virtio Mouse") and
    the tablet is event5.  evtest was started on BOTH -- the tablet as well,
    so that the switch shows up as one going quiet while the other speaks.

NOT MEASURED BY THIS AUTHOR: the evtest output, and `info mice` after a grab.
The machine was returned before a human took the grab, so the REL_X/REL_Y count
that patches/0007 asks for still has not been read by anyone.

WHAT THE USER REPORTED: pointer lock works.  That is the outcome 0008 and this
patch were for.  It is a person's report rather than a measurement -- but it is
not a weak one, because pointer lock cannot work at all while an absolute
device is current: SDL never asks the compositor for the lock in that state.  A
working lock therefore implies the switch happened.  The same session reported
it as "slow"; the input-latency suspect is item 3 of "IF IT IS SLOW" in
patches/0008, and it is SDL's poll cadence, not this patch.

Edge cases, none of them measured either:

  * NO RELATIVE DEVICE, the default tablet-only config: the lookup returns 0 and
    grab never calls qemu_mouse_set().  Not an error path -- byte for byte the
    old behaviour, in both directions;
  * ALREADY RELATIVE when the grab is taken: nothing to switch, nothing saved;
  * NO ABSOLUTE DEVICE: ungrab finds no candidate and leaves the guest alone,
    which is right for a relative-only guest;
  * RESTORE WHAT WAS CURRENT, not merely "an absolute device".  Grab saves the
    current index and ungrab re-activates that exact one, saved only when it was
    absolute.  With nothing saved, or the saved device unplugged since, ungrab
    falls back to any absolute device;
  * GUEST WENT ABSOLUTE MID-GRAB: ungrab sees an absolute device already current
    and leaves the guest's choice alone;
  * TWO DEVICES OF A KIND: take the one closest to the head of the handler list,
    i.e. the most recently activated.  qmp_query_mice() prepends, so that is the
    LAST match in its list, which is why the loop overwrites instead of stopping.

KNOWN GAP, left in the open rather than papered over: sdl_grab_start()'s
guest_cursor branch does not call sdl_hide_cursor() at all, so a guest that has
defined a hardware cursor gets the device switch without the pointer lock.
nvkvm's console never defines one -- nvkvm_present_hwops has no cursor ops -- so
it does not arise here, and fixing it means reworking upstream's cursor handling.

THE GRAB IS STILL A USER GESTURE.  Nothing here initiates one; it only changes
what a grab the user already took does.  Note also that upstream's implicit grab
in handle_mousebutton() (left button up while the current device is relative)
becomes unreachable in nvkvm's configuration once this patch is in: ungrab always
restores an absolute device, so the guest cannot arrange the precondition.  It
stays live only for a relative-only guest, which is the case upstream means it
for.  That is why there is no ui/sdl2.c twin of patches/0006.

Only compiled into a build configured --enable-sdl, i.e. NVKVM_QEMU_UI=1.  GTK
is untouched.

HOW TO ACTUALLY VERIFY, on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU and a Wayland display:

    NVKVM_QEMU_UI=1 scripts/build_qemu.sh --force
    VM_RELATIVE_MOUSE=1 VM_DISPLAY="sdl,gl=on" VM_SERIAL=none \
        scripts/run_test_vm.sh

  VM_RELATIVE_MOUSE=1 is required: without a virtio-mouse there is no relative
  device to switch to and this patch is a deliberate no-op.

  * host side: `ls -l /proc/$(pgrep -f qemu-system-x86_64)/fd | grep -c wayland`
    must be non-zero (SDL is a real Wayland client; GTK scored 0 here);
  * monitor `info mice` before the grab: the tablet is starred.  Take the grab
    with Ctrl+Alt+G; `info mice` now stars the virtio-mouse.  Release it; the
    tablet is starred again.  That alone is what this patch does;
  * in the guest, while grabbed:
        sudo evtest /dev/input/by-path/pci-0000:00:09.0-event-mouse
    and move the mouse.  REL_X/REL_Y events mean the whole QEMU-side chain
    works.  Silence means the break is at or below virtio-input and this patch
    is looking in the right place but is not enough -- exactly the split
    patches/0007 asks for, now on a host where the lock is real;
  * the cursor must not escape the window while grabbed, and must come back
    under host control after Ctrl+Alt+G.  A pointer that stays locked after
    release means the ungrab ordering above is wrong, and is the most likely
    way this patch fails;
  * with the default tablet-only config (no VM_RELATIVE_MOUSE) nothing may
    change: grab and release must behave exactly as before.

NOT UPSTREAMABLE AS WRITTEN.  The gap is general -- upstream's grab is a
host-side window concept with no opinion about which guest device should be
current, and this is now the second front end carrying its own copy of the
answer.  The upstreamable version is the shared helper in ui/input.c that
patches/0007 already asks for, with the mode choice expressed once instead of
per backend.  Two copies is the argument for writing it.
---
 ui/sdl2.c | 166 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 166 insertions(+)

diff --git a/ui/sdl2.c b/ui/sdl2.c
index 6b207e5..687535c 100644
--- a/ui/sdl2.c
+++ b/ui/sdl2.c
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
 #include "ui/console.h"
 #include "ui/input.h"
 #include "ui/sdl2.h"
+#include "qapi/qapi-commands-ui.h"
 #include "sysemu/runstate.h"
 #include "sysemu/runstate-action.h"
 #include "sysemu/sysemu.h"
@@ -242,6 +243,140 @@ static void sdl_show_cursor(struct sdl2_console *scon)
     SDL_ShowCursor(SDL_ENABLE);
 }
 
+/*
+ * nvkvm: make the grab decide which pointing device the guest sees.
+ *
+ * SDL_SetRelativeMouseMode() is the only real pointer lock QEMU has -- on a
+ * Wayland host SDL2 implements it with zwp_pointer_constraints_v1 +
+ * zwp_relative_pointer_manager_v1, the same mechanism a native game uses -- and
+ * sdl_hide_cursor() below asks for it only when !qemu_input_is_absolute().
+ * With nvkvm's default virtio-tablet current that is FALSE, so the grab
+ * confines the cursor and nothing else, and a guest waiting on deltas gets
+ * none.  So: on grab, activate a relative device; on ungrab, put the absolute
+ * one back.
+ *
+ * This is the ui/sdl2.c twin of patches/0007.  That patch's header records a
+ * negative result -- the same switch done by hand did not produce guest motion
+ * under GTK.  It does not transfer: GTK never takes a Wayland lock at all
+ * (gd_grab_pointer() does gdk_seat_grab() plus a warp, the pre-Wayland way of
+ * emulating one), so on that host there were no deltas to deliver whatever
+ * device was current.  Under SDL the lock is real and the device switch is
+ * exactly the missing half.  That is an argument, not a measurement: see the
+ * commit message for what has and has not been observed.
+ *
+ * Both directions go through the public interface -- qmp_query_mice() to read
+ * the list, qemu_mouse_set() to activate -- which is what the monitor's
+ * `info mice` and `mouse_set` do, so nothing new is exported and the monitor
+ * sees the same state.
+ */
+
+/* Index of the absolute device to restore on ungrab; 0 = nothing saved. */
+static int nvkvm_sdl_saved_mouse;
+
+/*
+ * Set while WE are activating a device, so sdl_mouse_mode_change() can tell
+ * our own switch from the guest's.  Without it the notifier fires re-entrantly
+ * from inside qemu_input_handler_activate() and runs the grab side effects:
+ * going relative would sdl_grab_end() the grab we are in the middle of taking,
+ * and going absolute would absolute_mouse_grab() a new one as we release.
+ * Worse, both act on sdl2_console[0] -- the boot VGA's window, not the guest's.
+ */
+static bool nvkvm_sdl_switching;
+
+/*
+ * One walk over the mouse list.  Returns the index of the device to make
+ * current for `absolute`, or 0 when the guest has none; *cur / *cur_abs
+ * describe whichever device is current right now (*cur is 0 when the guest has
+ * no mouse at all).
+ *
+ * qmp_query_mice() marks the HEAD of QEMU's handler list current and builds its
+ * answer by PREPENDING, so the list it hands back runs tail-to-head: the match
+ * closest to the head -- the most recently activated one -- is the LAST match
+ * in the list, not the first.  That is why the loop overwrites `found` instead
+ * of stopping.
+ */
+static int nvkvm_sdl_pick_mouse(bool absolute, int *cur, bool *cur_abs)
+{
+    MouseInfoList *mice, *entry;
+    int found = 0;
+
+    *cur = 0;
+    *cur_abs = false;
+
+    mice = qmp_query_mice(NULL);
+    for (entry = mice; entry != NULL; entry = entry->next) {
+        MouseInfo *m = entry->value;
+
+        if (m->current) {
+            *cur = (int)m->index;
+            *cur_abs = m->absolute;
+        }
+        if (m->absolute == absolute) {
+            found = (int)m->index;
+        }
+    }
+    qapi_free_MouseInfoList(mice);
+
+    return found;
+}
+
+static void nvkvm_sdl_grab_set_relative(void)
+{
+    bool cur_abs = false;
+    int cur = 0;
+    int rel;
+
+    nvkvm_sdl_saved_mouse = 0;
+
+    rel = nvkvm_sdl_pick_mouse(false, &cur, &cur_abs);
+    if (rel == 0 || rel == cur) {
+        /*
+         * No relative device at all -- the default tablet-only config -- or the
+         * guest already has one current.  Change nothing, so the grab behaves
+         * exactly as it did before this patch.
+         */
+        return;
+    }
+
+    nvkvm_sdl_switching = true;
+    if (qemu_mouse_set(rel, NULL)) {
+        /* Only an absolute device is worth restoring by index. */
+        nvkvm_sdl_saved_mouse = cur_abs ? cur : 0;
+    }
+    nvkvm_sdl_switching = false;
+}
+
+static void nvkvm_sdl_ungrab_set_absolute(void)
+{
+    bool cur_abs = false;
+    int cur = 0;
+    int want = nvkvm_sdl_saved_mouse;
+    int abs_idx;
+
+    nvkvm_sdl_saved_mouse = 0;
+
+    abs_idx = nvkvm_sdl_pick_mouse(true, &cur, &cur_abs);
+    if (cur_abs) {
+        /* The guest went absolute on its own while we held the grab; leave its
+         * choice alone. */
+        return;
+    }
+
+    nvkvm_sdl_switching = true;
+    if (want == 0 || !qemu_mouse_set(want, NULL)) {
+        /*
+         * Nothing was saved (the grab found the guest already relative), or the
+         * saved device has been unplugged since.  Fall back to any absolute
+         * device; with none -- a relative-only guest -- leave the guest alone,
+         * as before.
+         */
+        if (abs_idx != 0 && abs_idx != want) {
+            qemu_mouse_set(abs_idx, NULL);
+        }
+    }
+    nvkvm_sdl_switching = false;
+}
+
 static void sdl_grab_start(struct sdl2_console *scon)
 {
     QemuConsole *con = scon ? scon->dcl.con : NULL;
@@ -257,6 +392,20 @@ static void sdl_grab_start(struct sdl2_console *scon)
     if (!(SDL_GetWindowFlags(scon->real_window) & SDL_WINDOW_INPUT_FOCUS)) {
         return;
     }
+    /*
+     * nvkvm: BEFORE the cursor decision below, because sdl_hide_cursor() asks
+     * for relative mode only when the current device is relative -- so the
+     * switch has to have happened by the time it runs.
+     *
+     * Known gap, deliberately not papered over: the guest_cursor branch below
+     * does not call sdl_hide_cursor() at all, so a guest that has defined a
+     * hardware cursor gets the device switch without the pointer lock.
+     * nvkvm's console never defines one (nvkvm_present_hwops has no cursor
+     * ops), so this does not arise here; fixing it properly means reworking
+     * upstream's cursor handling, which is a different patch.
+     */
+    nvkvm_sdl_grab_set_relative();
+
     if (guest_cursor) {
         SDL_SetCursor(guest_sprite);
         if (!qemu_input_is_absolute(scon->dcl.con) && !absolute_enabled) {
@@ -277,6 +426,13 @@ static void sdl_grab_end(struct sdl2_console *scon)
     gui_grab = 0;
     win32_kbd_set_grab(false);
     sdl_show_cursor(scon);
+    /*
+     * nvkvm: AFTER sdl_show_cursor(), which leaves relative mode only while the
+     * current device is still relative.  Restoring the tablet first would make
+     * its !qemu_input_is_absolute() test false and the pointer would stay
+     * locked with no way out.
+     */
+    nvkvm_sdl_ungrab_set_absolute();
     sdl_update_caption(scon);
 }
 
@@ -294,6 +450,16 @@ static void absolute_mouse_grab(struct sdl2_console *scon)
 
 static void sdl_mouse_mode_change(Notifier *notify, void *data)
 {
+    if (nvkvm_sdl_switching) {
+        /*
+         * nvkvm: this activation is OURS, from inside sdl_grab_start() or
+         * sdl_grab_end().  Track the mode, but do not run the grab side
+         * effects -- they would undo the grab transition in progress, and they
+         * would do it to console 0's window rather than the guest's.
+         */
+        absolute_enabled = qemu_input_is_absolute(sdl2_console[0].dcl.con);
+        return;
+    }
     if (qemu_input_is_absolute(sdl2_console[0].dcl.con)) {
         if (!absolute_enabled) {
             absolute_enabled = 1;
